WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.590 --> 00:00:07.180
So, this is a sample of Rubidium. It arrived
just a couple of days ago, from Alpha the

00:00:07.180 --> 00:00:13.540
company, so there is one gram of Rubidium,
it costs about £65. It is one of the alkali

00:00:13.540 --> 00:00:18.090
metals, so it is very, very reactive, a bit
like sodium but perhaps a little bit more.

00:00:18.090 --> 00:00:23.539
So the metal itself is quite low melting,
it won’t melt in my hand though unfortunately,

00:00:23.539 --> 00:00:27.380
so I am not going to show you it liquid now,
but you can see there is one gram of the metal

00:00:27.380 --> 00:00:32.469
inside this glass ampoule. It’s very reactive,
and we are going to explore its chemistry

00:00:32.469 --> 00:00:38.120
now by putting this into some water. Well
we’ve got this really neat device which

00:00:38.120 --> 00:00:43.620
Neil put together last Friday and you can
see it’s got a hammer which is going to

00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:44.620
break the glass.

00:00:44.620 --> 00:00:49.300
Show us Neil, what does it do mate?

00:00:49.300 --> 00:00:55.079
So when the hammer falls, literally, we will
break the glass, the rubidium will just drop

00:00:55.079 --> 00:00:57.500
into the big bucket of water that we’ve
got underneath.

00:00:57.500 --> 00:01:04.339
Rubidium is an alkali metal that comes between
potassium and caesium and both potassium and

00:01:04.339 --> 00:01:12.819
caesium are much better known among chemists
than rubidium. Potassium because it is all

00:01:12.819 --> 00:01:19.009
over the world, the environment, you have
potassium in cells and plants and so on, I

00:01:19.009 --> 00:01:25.959
mean biological cells. Rubidium on the other
hand is quite rare.

00:01:25.959 --> 00:01:29.740
So I am going to give the sample now to Neil
and Neil is going to load it and we are going

00:01:29.740 --> 00:01:32.950
to drop the hammer on it. There you go Neil.

00:01:32.950 --> 00:01:40.469
It was discovered originally by the German
chemist Bunsen or ‘Bunsen’ as he was called,

00:01:40.469 --> 00:01:46.630
the one who invented the Bunsen burner and
he discovered it by looking at the colours

00:01:46.630 --> 00:01:54.209
that you see in flames, when he was heating
up water that came from, mineral water that

00:01:54.209 --> 00:02:00.630
came from springs somewhere in Germany I believe,
or it may have been somewhere in middle Europe.

00:02:00.630 --> 00:02:06.929
So Neil is arranging the sample of Rubidium
against the metallic back plate in the small

00:02:06.929 --> 00:02:08.429
metallic collider.

00:02:08.429 --> 00:02:16.099
Rubidium gives a characteristic colour that
is different from lilac of sodium or, I’m

00:02:16.099 --> 00:02:20.349
sorry, lilac of potassium or the orange of
sodium.

00:02:20.349 --> 00:02:24.519
The rubidium is slightly more reactive than
sodium so when we did the sodium experiments,

00:02:24.519 --> 00:02:29.700
we had our safety glasses and coat on, but
for rubidium we are going to wear secondary

00:02:29.700 --> 00:02:36.040
goggles, so here’s my safety goggles, which
I am going to put on top. Don’t we look

00:02:36.040 --> 00:02:37.040
good.

00:02:37.040 --> 00:02:45.150
Nowadays, one of the main uses of rubidium,
is actually in physics rather than in chemistry

00:02:45.150 --> 00:02:51.640
because you can heat up rubidium metal to
a high temperature and get a vapour of rubidium

00:02:51.640 --> 00:02:58.719
atoms in a specially constructed heated cell
and you can get very interesting effects from

00:02:58.719 --> 00:03:05.810
shining very intense laser light into rubidium
atoms, and the rubidium atoms can do a whole

00:03:05.810 --> 00:03:11.890
series of different, very elegant physics
experiments. So rubidium has got a life of

00:03:11.890 --> 00:03:18.130
its own in physics but in chemistry, it’s
perhaps slightly neglected.

00:03:18.130 --> 00:03:30.129
Was that it?

00:03:30.129 --> 00:03:35.480
Don’t think I needed my safety screen.

00:03:35.480 --> 00:03:59.459
Well that wasn't very exciting was it?

00:03:59.459 --> 00:04:06.599
There still could be some in there.

00:04:06.599 --> 00:04:08.909
It's all gone.

00:04:08.909 --> 00:04:16.120
You were right it was just a fizzle.

